Observation (1) carries over to NR: Btm-20 sectors again show the largest gains, exceeding 80% for the top-ranked changes, compared with about 25% for Mid-60 and at most about 7% for Top-20 sectors. RAN-NFM-Pro achieves the highest or near-highest gains in the Btm-20 group, where the gains are largest and optimization matters most to operators, while NetLLM yields noticeably lower gains up to about the top 25%. In the Mid-60 group, RAN-NFM-Pro trails NetLLM and Auric-XGB among the top 6% of changes and reaches comparable gains beyond that point. RAN-NFM-Core stays slightly below RAN-NFM-Pro and Auric-XGB in both groups. In the Top-20 group, NetLLM achieves the highest gains, but the gains of all models remain below about 3% beyond the top 1%, consistent with their limited headroom.

Figure 6(h) shows the AUC as a function of the throughput range. Consistent with LTE, the benefit is concentrated in the lowest-throughput sectors: the AUC in the bottom 10% range is about 3.5$\times$ that in the next range and drops close to zero above the 60th percentile. In the bottom 10% range, RAN-NFM-Pro achieves the highest AUC, closely followed by RAN-NFM-Core and Auric-XGB, whereas NetLLM achieves the highest or near-highest AUC in the 40%--50% range and above the 70th percentile, in line with Figures 6(f) and 6(g).

Unlike on LTE, the foundation models do not outperform Auric-XGB by a large margin on NR. A likely reason is that none of the NR configuration features appear in the LTE pretraining data (Section 4.2). The NR models can reuse the pretrained KPI encoder, but the representations of the configuration features, which determine the effect of a configuration change, are learned from the limited NR labels alone. In this setting, XGBoost on engineered features remains a strong baseline.
